  'Exploring Watamu: A Hidden Gem North of Mombasa',
  'exploring-watamu-hidden-gem',
  'Discover Watamu, a quieter alternative to Diani with stunning marine parks, pristine beaches, and incredible biodiversity.',
  E'# Exploring Watamu: A Hidden Gem North of Mombasa\n\nWhile Diani gets most of the attention, Watamu offers an equally stunning but more tranquil coastal experience. This small town has become a favorite among travelers seeking authentic beauty without the crowds.\n\n## The Marine National Park\n\nWatamu Marine National Park is one of the oldest in Kenya, established in 1968. The protected waters host:\n\n- Over 600 fish species\n- Sea turtles (5 different species)\n- Dolphins and occasional whale sharks\n- Pristine coral gardens\n- Crystal-clear waters perfect for snorkeling\n\n## Gede Ruins\n\nJust inland, the mysterious Gede Ruins offer a glimpse into Swahili coastal history. This 13th-century town was mysteriously abandoned, leaving behind fascinating architectural remains set in a lush forest.\n\n## Bio-Ken Snake Farm\n\nFor the adventurous, Bio-Ken offers educational tours about Kenya''s snakes and produces anti-venom for the region. It''s both fascinating and educational.\n\n## Beach Life\n\nWatamu''s beaches are quieter than Diani but equally beautiful. The famous rock pools at low tide create natural swimming pools perfect for families. The sunsets here are legendary.\n\n## Where to Stay\n\nWe offer several beautiful properties in Watamu:\n- Beachfront villas with private access\n- Eco-lodges in the forest\n- Comfortable apartments in town\n\n## Best Time to Visit\n\nVisit between October and March for the best weather and sea conditions. The green season (April-June) offers lower rates and fewer crowds.\n\n## Getting There\n\nWatamu is 105km north of Mombasa, about a 2-hour drive. Regular matatus and private transfers are available.',
  'Destinations',
  ARRAY['Watamu', 'Beach', 'Marine Park', 'Travel', 'Kenya'],
