(
  'Coastal Cuisine: A Food Lover''s Guide to Kenyan Beach Food',
  'coastal-cuisine-food-lovers-guide',
  'From fresh seafood to Swahili spices, explore the incredible flavors of Kenya''s coastal cuisine and where to find the best local dishes.',
  E'# Coastal Cuisine: A Food Lover''s Guide to Kenyan Beach Food\n\nKenyan coastal cuisine is a delicious fusion of African, Arab, and Indian influences, creating unique flavors you won''t find anywhere else.\n\n## Must-Try Dishes\n\n### Pwani (Coastal) Pilau\nFragrant rice cooked with spices, often served with coconut fish or chicken. The aromatic blend of cinnamon, cardamom, and cloves is unforgettable.\n\n### Coconut Fish Curry (Samaki wa Nazi)\nFresh catch of the day cooked in rich coconut milk with tomatoes and spices. Best enjoyed with ugali or rice.\n\n### Urojo (Soup)\nA tangy, spicy soup with bhajias, cassava, and potatoes. A favorite street food in Mombasa.\n\n### Mkate wa Sinia\nSweet, cardamom-flavored bread baked in round pans. Perfect for breakfast with chai.\n\n### Fresh Seafood\nLobster, prawns, calamari, and countless fish varieties prepared to perfection with local spices.\n\n## Best Places to Eat\n\n### Local Restaurants\nTry Ali Barbour''s Cave Restaurant in Diani for a unique dining experience in a natural coral cave.\n\n### Street Food\nExplore Old Town Mombasa for authentic street food and local eateries.\n\n### Beachfront Dining\nMany beach bars serve fresh catch of the day grilled to order.\n\n### Home Cooking\nBook a homestay for the most authentic experience of coastal cooking.\n\n## Cooking Classes\n\nMany of our homestay hosts offer cooking classes where you can learn to prepare traditional Swahili dishes using local ingredients and techniques passed down through generations.\n\n## Food Safety Tips\n\n- Drink bottled water\n- Eat at busy, well-reviewed establishments\n- Try street food where locals eat\n- Be adventurous but listen to your body',
  'Culture',
